Morris preorder
Preorder needs the node recorded on the first arrival instead of the second. So move the push_back
into the branch that creates the thread:
vector<int> morrisPreorder(TreeNode* root) {
vector<int> out;
TreeNode* curr = root;
while (curr != NULL) {
if (curr->left == NULL) {
out.push_back(curr->val);
curr = curr->right;
} else {
TreeNode* pred = curr->left;
while (pred->right != NULL && pred->right != curr)
pred = pred->right;
if (pred->right == NULL) {
out.push_back(curr->val); // record on the FIRST visit
pred->right = curr;
curr = curr->left;
} else {
pred->right = NULL;
curr = curr->right;
}
}
}

Every path is counted exactly once, at its own turning point. Nothing is missed and nothing is double
counted.
Brute force, and why it is slow
Translate the formula directly:
int height(TreeNode* root) {
if (root == NULL) return 0;
return 1 + max(height(root->left), height(root->right));
}
int diameterBrute(TreeNode* root) {
if (root == NULL) return 0;
int through = height(root->left) + height(root->right); // path turning here
int left = diameterBrute(root->left); // best path entirely on the left
int right = diameterBrute(root->right); // best path entirely on the right
return max(through, max(left, right));
}
This is correct. It is also wasteful. At every node we call height, which walks the entire subtree below
that node. We computed those heights already inside the outer recursion and threw them away .
